WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.950
Join tables, and prove your numbers add up.

2
00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:09.790
This is the video for module 2 of SQL Fundamentals: Tools, Joins and Trustworthy Results.

3
00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:18.570
You learn SQL by typing queries and seeing results. You do not need to buy anything. Choose one tool and stay with it.

4
00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:24.510
SQLite Fiddle runs in any browser, even on your phone, with no account.

5
00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:30.600
DB Browser for SQLite works offline once installed on a laptop.

6
00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:38.980
A free PostgreSQL project, for example on Supabase, lets you practise the database many fintechs use.

7
00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:50.060
And the BigQuery sandbox lets you query large public datasets. Whichever you choose, keep your queries in a notes file. It becomes your query library.

8
00:00:51.310 --> 00:01:01.550
For example, Ada studies on her phone at lunch. She opens SQLite Fiddle and runs the CREATE TABLE statements for customers, orders and order items.

9
00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:08.880
She pastes the INSERT statements: 6 customers, 10 orders and 13 order item rows.

10
00:01:10.130 --> 00:01:14.760
Then she counts the rows in each table. 6, 10 and 13. Correct.

11
00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:30.480
She sums the amount for all 10 orders and gets 251,500, as expected. Why check? A missing comma can stop one INSERT while the others succeed, and then every later answer is wrong.

12
00:01:31.730 --> 00:01:43.210
She saves the script so she can rebuild any time. And remember, use made-up data for practice. Never put real customer names or phone numbers into a public online tool.

13
00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:57.580
A JOIN lines up rows from two tables wherever the key values match. An INNER JOIN keeps only the matches. Joining delivered orders to customers gives 8 rows, each now with a name and city.

14
00:01:58.830 --> 00:02:11.990
A LEFT JOIN keeps every row of the first table, even with no match. The missing columns come back as NULL. That is how you find who is missing, like Funmi, a customer who has never received an order.

15
00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:21.090
She starts from customers, so nobody is lost, and puts status equals delivered inside the ON part of the join, not in WHERE.

16
00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:36.060
Dupe spent 61,000 naira, Amaka 55,000, Bisi 54,500, Efe 33,000 and Chioma 8,000. Funmi has 0, so she gets the welcome offer.

17
00:02:37.310 --> 00:02:42.110
It reconciles to 211,500, the delivered total.

18
00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:51.540
The common mistake: if she had put the delivered filter in WHERE, Funmi's row would vanish, and the LEFT JOIN would quietly act like an INNER JOIN.

19
00:02:52.790 --> 00:03:04.100
A query that runs without error is not always right. Ngozi sums the order amount after joining to order items, and gets 293,500.

20
00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:09.830
But the known delivered total is 211,500.

21
00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:22.880
The gap is 82,000. After the join there are 11 rows, not 8, because three orders have two product lines each, so each is counted twice. This is called fan-out.

22
00:03:23.230 --> 00:03:31.780
The quick test: compare COUNT star with COUNT DISTINCT order ID. If the first grows and the second does not, rows are repeating.

23
00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:39.620
Write the question in one sentence, with a time period and a definition, like delivered revenue per city.

24
00:03:40.870 --> 00:03:44.070
Explore the tables first, and check which keys link them.

25
00:03:45.320 --> 00:03:52.340
Build the query in small pieces. Run the join first and check the row count, then add WHERE, then GROUP BY.

26
00:03:53.590 --> 00:03:57.090
Reconcile to a known total, and check two rows by hand.

27
00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:02.330
Then present the number, the period and any caveat in plain words.

28
00:04:03.580 --> 00:04:18.770
Five checks catch most silent errors. Does the row count make sense? Do totals reconcile? Is there fan-out? Are NULLs handled on purpose, for example with COALESCE to turn a missing value into 0? And did you spot-check two rows?

29
00:04:20.020 --> 00:04:32.990
To remember. Check counts and totals as soon as data is loaded. Use INNER JOIN for what was sold and LEFT JOIN for who is missing. Watch for fan-out. And never share a number you have not reconciled.

30
00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:40.710
Now build and verify your practice database, answer three join questions, and audit a wrong query.

31
00:04:41.060 --> 00:04:43.000
Continue with the lessons below.
